Point the help browser at automod, and unstick the doc it orphaned

The catalog matches entries to commands by name, so renaming ,filter left a doc
called `filter` behind -- and because exactly one command still answered to that
name it was adopted unconditionally. ,boosterrole filter has been wearing the
word filter's documentation and sitting in the Filters section of ,help. Nothing
errored and every total stayed right, because the doc was still attached to
something.

The entry is now `automod` in a section called AutoMod, its subcommand list
rewritten from the six it used to claim to the seventeen there actually are, and
the description says which of ignore and whitelist takes text. inCategory() was
renamed to match. ,boosterrole filter is back under Booster roles with no doc of
its own, which is what it always should have had.

The stale `snipe` doc and its empty section went too; the command left with the
utility cog and the doc had been describing `,filter snipe` ever since.

docaudit now checks every doc entry and every section individually, 268 checks
up from 94: a doc no command answers to, and a section nothing files under.
That catches the orphan, not the adoption -- the sharper test, a command's own
inCategory() slug disagreeing with its doc's, already has three deliberate
exceptions and would cry wolf every run.

